# The soak candidate: the commit the scheduled lanes certify.
#
# **What it is for.** The layer exits on five consecutive qualifying runs of
# `.github/workflows/soak.yml` over one frozen candidate. A lane that checked
# out the default branch's head would mint a new candidate on every merge, and
# the count would then be held by nobody merging — a discipline no file records
# and no run attests. So the candidate is pinned here: both scheduled jobs read
# this pointer off the default branch, check that commit out, and stamp it into
# the qualification record they upload.
#
# **Editing it starts a new count.** That is the whole point of the edit, and it
# is a reviewed commit like any other. Nothing here retires the records already
# written: they name the candidate they ran, so a campaign reading them sees the
# candidate change rather than inheriting it.
#
# **A run whose tree is not this commit still writes a record**, classified
# `candidate-mismatch` — the pointer unreadable, the checkout refused, or a
# lane's `ref:` gone missing all leave a run certifying some other commit, and
# the record says which one.
#
# **Format.** `#` comments, then one line holding a 40-digit lowercase commit
# sha. The reading step in both jobs of soak.yml refuses anything else, and the
# shape is held by `norn --test certification`.
#
# This file is outside the trees the suite manifest sweeps: what the suite is
# and which commit it is run against are two values, and folding the pointer
# into the first would restart the count every time the second advanced.
